$\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=3x^2-6x-9\\&=3(x+1)(x-3)\end{aligned}$

The derivative is zero at $x=-1$ and $x=3$. Its parabola opens upwards, so $f'(x)>0$ outside the roots and $f'(x)<0$ between them.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{derivativeRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[->,black] (-2.2,0)--(4.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-1.5)--(0,2.2) node[above] {$f'(x)$};
\draw[thick,derivativeRed,<->,domain=-1.9:3.9,samples=81,variable=\x] plot (\x,{0.35*(\x+1)*(\x-3)});
\fill[black] (-1,0) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[black] (3,0) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-1,0) {$-1$};
\node[below right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (3,0) {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Increasing: $x<-1$ or $x>3$.

Decreasing: $-1<x<3$.

The curve is stationary at $x=-1$ and $x=3$.

$\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=9+6x-3x^2\\&=-3(x+1)(x-3)\end{aligned}$

The derivative is zero at $x=-1$ and $x=3$. Its parabola opens downwards, so the derivative is positive between the roots and negative outside them.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{derivativeRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[->,black] (-2.2,0)--(4.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-2.2)--(0,1.8) node[above] {$f'(x)$};
\draw[thick,derivativeRed,<->,domain=-1.9:3.9,samples=81,variable=\x] plot (\x,{-0.35*(\x+1)*(\x-3)});
\fill[black] (-1,0) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[black] (3,0) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-1,0) {$-1$};
\node[below right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (3,0) {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Increasing: $-1<x<3$.

Decreasing: $x<-1$ or $x>3$.

The curve is stationary at $x=-1$ and $x=3$.
